{#
 # Framework demo: tenancy "Tenants in Queued Jobs" feature.
 # Migrated to the ShowcaseKit feature layout via _tenancy-feature wrapper.
 # Bespoke L1/L2/L3 markup captured into previewHtml / l2Content / l3Content.
 #}
{% set title = 'Tenants in Queued Jobs' %}
{% set summary = 'The core queue does not carry the tenant. A Queued handler takes the tenant id from its payload and enters that tenant itself.' %}

{% set previewHtml %}
<div class="feature-l1">
    <p class="feature-entry">The core queue does not carry the tenant. The flow below is <code>TenantAwareJobSerializer</code>, a helper for a package that owns both its dispatcher and its worker. In the framework only media uses it.</p>

    <div class="queue-viz">
        <div class="queue-viz__producer">
            <h4>Package dispatcher (request context)</h4>
            <div class="code-block"><pre>// Dispatch while tenant = '{{ tenantContext.org }}'
TenantAwareJobSerializer::wrap([
    'action' => 'generate_report',
    'product_id' => 42,
]);
// adds _tenant from the current context
// adds nothing in the default tenant</pre></div>
        </div>

        <div class="queue-viz__arrow">→ queue →</div>

        <div class="queue-viz__payload">
            <h4>Serialized payload</h4>
            <div class="code-block"><pre>{{ serializedPayload|json_encode(constant('JSON_PRETTY_PRINT')) }}</pre></div>
        </div>

        <div class="queue-viz__arrow">→ worker →</div>

        <div class="queue-viz__worker">
            <h4>Package worker</h4>
            {% for step in workerSteps %}
                <div class="worker-step">
                    <span class="worker-step__num">{{ step.step }}</span>
                    <div>
                        <code>{{ step.label }}</code>
                        <div class="muted">{{ step.detail }}</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
{% endset %}

{% set l2Content %}
<div class="feature-l2">
    <h3>A Queued handler enters the tenant itself</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><pre>use Semitexa\Tenancy\Context\TenantContext;
use Semitexa\Tenancy\Context\TenantContextStore;

// Before dispatching: the payload carries the tenant id.
$request->setTenantId(TenantContext::get()?->getTenantId());

// In the Queued handler:
public function handle(SendInvoice $request, SendInvoiceResult $response): SendInvoiceResult
{
    $store = TenantContextStore::shared();
    $store->clear(); // set() locks the context inside a coroutine; clear() drops the lock
    $store->set(TenantContext::fromResolution($request->getTenantId(), 'queue'));
    try {
        // tenant-scoped work
    } finally {
        $store->clear();
    }

    return $response;
}</pre></div>
    <p><code>QueueDispatcher</code> writes no tenant into the message and <code>QueueWorker</code> restores none. Give the payload a nullable <code>tenantId</code> with a public getter and setter. The id is <code>null</code> with no tenant context and <code>'default'</code> in the default tenant, so skip the tenant switch for those. <code>fromResolution()</code> builds the organization layer only. Carry the locale or environment in the payload too if the job needs them.</p>

    <h3>TenantAwareJobSerializer: for a package with its own worker</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><pre>use Semitexa\Tenancy\Application\Service\TenantAwareJobSerializer;

// The package's dispatcher (MediaQueueDispatcher does this):
$message = TenantAwareJobSerializer::wrap(['action' => 'generate_report', 'product_id' => 42]);
// ['action' => ..., '_tenant' => ['tenantId' => 'acme', 'strategy' => ...,
//     'locale' => 'en', 'environment' => ...]]

// The package's worker (MediaWorker does this):
$data = TenantAwareJobSerializer::unwrapAndRestore($message);
// $data has no _tenant key; the context is set as the store's fallback

// Or take the context without making it current:
[$data, $context] = TenantAwareJobSerializer::unwrap($message);
// $context is a TenantContext, or null when there was no _tenant key</pre></div>
    <p>All three methods are static. In the default tenant <code>wrap()</code> adds nothing. The store's fallback is read only outside a coroutine. Wrapping the payload of a <code>Queued</code> handler's request does nothing useful: nothing on the core worker's side unwraps it.</p>
</div>
{% endset %}

{% set l3Content %}
<div class="feature-l3">
    <h3>Why this matters for SaaS</h3>
    <p>A queued job that runs in the default scope reads and writes the wrong data without failing. A report job for Acme does not see Acme's rows, and nothing warns about it.</p>
    <p>There is no automatic way yet. Every queued job that touches tenant-scoped data must name its tenant in the payload and enter it before the first query. The worker wipes the tenant context after every message, so a job that forgets <code>clear()</code> does not leave its tenant to the next one.</p>
    <p>For work on a clock, a scheduled job with <code>per_tenant</code> gets this done for it: <code>scheduler:work</code> enters the run's tenant for the length of <code>handle()</code>.</p>
</div>
{% endset %}

{% extends '@project-layouts-semitexa-demo/platform/_tenancy-feature.html.twig' %}
